---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Media from '../components/Media.astro';

const skills = [
  {
    name: 'user research',
    what: 'Gathers user signals from trusted communities like Reddit, on the products I work on and on ones I’m only curious about, so a product call starts from what people say when nobody is asking them.',
  },
  {
    name: 'loops and swarms',
    what: 'Splits a big task across a swarm of agents and keeps each one in a loop, working, checking and retrying until its part is done.',
  },
  {
    name: 'benchmark sessions',
    what: 'Runs my own benchmark sessions: the same prompts across a set of models, scored the same way each time, so a new model gets measured against the old ones on tasks I care about.',
  },
  {
    name: 'ste100 voice',
    what: 'Inspired by Andrej Karpathy, it has agents explain things in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English, the controlled language written for aircraft maintenance manuals, with one meaning per word and short, unambiguous sentences.',
  },
  {
    name: 'malak-style',
    what: 'An editor that checks my writing for em dashes, buzzwords, hedges, semicolon chains and machine rhythm, then fixes what it can. Every page on this site passed it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'session memory',
    what: 'Saves what an agent learned in one session and loads it into the next, so context survives between tools and days.',
  },
];

---

<Base
  title="how i use ai"
  description="Malak Ali's AI stack: Claude and GPT for the hard reasoning, local Qwen, Gemma and Phi models for bulk runs, agent swarms on an orchestrator built on Orca, Capy and Hermes harnesses set up for recursive self-improvement, her own skills, and the software that replaced half her subscriptions."
>
  <section>
    <h1 class="page-title">how i use ai</h1>
    <p class="lede">Frontier models for the hard reasoning, local models for bulk runs and private data, swarms of coding agents working in parallel, and harnesses that write and improve their own skills. I’ve replaced about half of my consumer subscriptions with software I built this way, from my own news digest and investing desk to daily games for my family.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <div class="case-hero">
      <div class="hero-frame">
        <Media media={{ kind: 'demo', name: 'ai-stack' }} label="My AI stack: models, router, agent swarm, harnesses, skills and what ships" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <div class="ai-grid">
      <article class="ai-block">
        <p class="eyebrow">01 · models</p>
        <h2>Frontier and local, routed per step</h2>
        <p>Claude and GPT do the hard reasoning and most of my coding, through Claude Code, Codex and GitHub Copilot CLI. Qwen, Gemma and Microsoft’s Phi run on my MacBook for bulk runs, evals and anything I’d rather keep off someone else’s server, and long jobs run overnight without an API bill. My <a href="/writing/arabic-dialects-benchmark/">Arabic dialects benchmark</a> ran four models through more than 900 prompts each on this laptop. A router in my pipeline kernel picks the model and the reasoning effort for each step.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ai-block">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 · pipelines</p>
        <h2>One kernel for everything that runs daily</h2>
        <p><code>lifeos</code> is a small Python kernel with no third-party dependencies that runs my daily pipelines under launchd. Each run pulls its sources in parallel, scores and ranks what it finds, hands the write-up to a model, and delivers a report, with an append-only record so every run can say what changed since the last one. My investing desk runs on it, and every pipeline shares one trace and eval layer. By default a run can only write local files, and messages are drafted, never sent.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ai-block">
        <p class="eyebrow">03 · orchestration</p>
        <h2>Loops and swarms on my own orchestrator</h2>
        <p>I run coding agents in parallel with an orchestrator I built on the open-source <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca">Orca</a>. Every agent gets its own git worktree and branch, so a whole swarm can work in one repo without colliding, and I review their branches the way I’d review a team’s pull requests. For bigger tasks a skill splits the work across agents that each run in a loop, working, checking and retrying until their part is done. This site was built in one of those worktrees.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ai-block">
        <p class="eyebrow">04 · harnesses</p>
        <h2>The best harnesses, set up for self-improvement</h2>
        <p>The harness around a model decides as much as the model does, so I use the strongest ones I can find. Claude Code, Codex and GitHub Copilot CLI handle my daily work. <a href="https://capy.ai">Capy</a> runs fleets of coding agents, each in its own cloud sandbox. <a href="https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent">Hermes</a>, from Nous Research, writes a skill when it works out how to do something new and refines it the next time a similar task comes up. I run Capy and Hermes for recursive self-improvement: agents that write, test and improve the tools they use, so each run starts ahead of the last.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ai-block wide-block">
        <p class="eyebrow">05 · skills</p>
        <h2>Skills I wrote myself</h2>
        <p>A skill is a folder of instructions and scripts an agent loads only when it needs them. The ones I reach for most:</p>
        <ul class="skill-list">
          {skills.map((skill) => (
            <li>
              <code>{skill.name}</code>
              <span>{skill.what}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="muted small">Others draft Substack posts in my voice and take a startup idea from research to a go or no-go call.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ai-block wide-block">
        <p class="eyebrow">06 · at work</p>
        <h2>The same habits at work</h2>
        <p>On <a href="/work/recall/">Recall</a> I prototyped with AI tools, shipped my own interfaces without engineering, and put them in front of users in studies I ran myself. At Microsoft Research I lead the evals that tell us whether <a href="/work/quine/">Quine</a> learned what we wanted it to, across mid-training and post-training, and those results set the roadmap.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>software instead of subscriptions</h2>
      <span class="mono muted">built for me and my family</span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3 class="card-title">An investing desk instead of a terminal</h3>
          <div class="card-meta">lifeos · SEC filings · daily brief</div>
          <p class="card-summary">Every morning QuantDesk pulls market data, reads SEC insider filings and checks the macro backdrop, scores each stock on seven factors, and has an AI analyst write a reasoned brief on my watchlist and holdings. It runs on free, public data instead of a research subscription.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3 class="card-title">My own news digest</h3>
          <div class="card-meta">my sources · one page</div>
          <p class="card-summary">The stories I follow, from sources I pick, summarized in one place, so keeping up with the news doesn’t mean paying for a stack of paywalls.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3 class="card-title">Budgeting without a budgeting app</h3>
          <div class="card-meta">saved in the browser</div>
          <p class="card-summary">My own budgeting and expense-splitting apps. I import card transactions, review each purchase, and settle up with family at the end. Everything is saved in the browser, not on someone else’s server.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3 class="card-title">This site</h3>
          <div class="card-meta">built in an orca worktree</div>
          <p class="card-summary">Built with coding agents in one of my worktrees, with every page run through my style skill before it shipped. The benchmark behind the Arabic dialects post ran on my laptop.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="card wide-card">
        <div class="games">
          <img src="/media/built/tiny-voyages.jpg" alt="Tiny Voyages: a daily geography game with a globe, a 3,750 km distance target and a 65% over-water condition." loading="lazy" />
          <img src="/media/built/skyward.jpg" alt="Skyward: a pixel-art daily trivia climb showing prompt 3 of 7 and an altitude meter." loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3 class="card-title">Our own NYT, without the paywall</h3>
          <div class="card-meta">daily games · built with Codex</div>
          <p class="card-summary">NYT-style daily puzzles, so my family gets a new game every morning without another subscription. <strong>Tiny Voyages</strong> is a geography game played on a globe: six untimed rounds, 600 points. <strong>Skyward</strong> is a daily trivia climb with 112 prompts and nearly 19,000 accepted answers.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .ai-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px) clamp(24px, 4vw, 56px);
  }

  .ai-block .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
  }

  .ai-block h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: var(--display-weight);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .ai-block p {
    margin: 0 0 0.8em;
  }

  .skill-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0 0 1em;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 14px clamp(24px, 4vw, 56px);
  }

  .skill-list li {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: 2px;
  }

  .skill-list code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }

  .skill-list span {
    font-size: 0.97rem;
  }

  .wide-card,
  .wide-block {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }

  .ai-block p > code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }

  .small {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }


  .games {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 2px;
    background: var(--line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .games img {
    width: 100%;
    height: clamp(220px, 32vw, 420px);
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: top;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .ai-grid,
    .skill-list {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
